From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 14:57:28 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: h713: the watchdog uses the A523 register layout

The node claimed the H6/A31 watchdog (control 0x10, config 0x14, mode
0x18, no key). The H713's is the key-protected A523 one: control 0x0c,
config 0x10, mode 0x14, writes carrying 0x16aa in the top half. U-Boot
found that on hardware and has driven it that way; the live registers
agree (read as A31, the mode word 0x1f would be a running watchdog with
a one-unit timeout, on a board that does not reset).

So Linux's sunxi-wdt has been writing the wrong registers: the reboot
watchdog systemd arms (RebootWatchdogSec=16s in the rootfs) never ran.
With the right layout it does, and the driver still stops it at the
kernel's reboot/halt/power-off notifier (watchdog_stop_on_reboot), so it
bounds the shutdown phase only and a power-off stays off.

U-Boot is moving to this device tree, which is what surfaced it.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -1519,10 +1519,15 @@
 			};
 		};
 
-		/* === Watchdog === */
+		/*
+		 * === Watchdog ===
+		 * The key-protected A523 register layout (control 0x0c, config
+		 * 0x10, mode 0x14, key 0x16aa), as U-Boot found on hardware --
+		 * not the H6/A31 one (0x10/0x14/0x18, no key).
+		 */
 		watchdog: watchdog@2051000 {
-			compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h6-wdt",
-				     "allwinner,sun6i-a31-wdt";
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h713-wdt",
+				     "allwinner,sun55i-a523-wdt";
 			reg = <0x02051000 0x20>;
 			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 46 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
 			clocks = <&osc24M>;
